  <section>
    <h4 style="border-left: 3px solid var(--info); padding-left: 8px; margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 10px; color: var(--text-primary); font-weight: 600;">Deep Conceptual Explanation</h4>
    <p>The <b>Cell</b> is the fundamental structural and functional unit of all living organisms. Based on nuclear organization, cells are classified into <b>Prokaryotes</b> (lacking a defined nucleus, e.g., Bacteria) and <b>Eukaryotes</b> (possessing a membrane-bound nucleus, e.g., Plants and Animals). The <b>Cell Theory</b>, proposed by [[Schleiden]] and [[Schwann]] and later refined by [[Rudolf Virchow]], posits that all living things are composed of cells and that all cells arise from pre-existing cells. Key organelles like the <b>Mitochondria</b> (powerhouse) and <b>Ribosomes</b> (protein factories) ensure cellular homeostasis.</p><p>Cell division occurs via two primary mechanisms: <b>Mitosis</b> and <b>Meiosis</b>. Mitosis is an equational division resulting in two genetically identical diploid daughter cells, essential for growth and tissue repair. It consists of Prophase, Metaphase, Anaphase, and Telophase. In contrast, Meiosis is a reductional division occurring in germ cells to produce four haploid gametes, ensuring genetic diversity through <b>Crossing Over</b> during Prophase I. This process is critical for sexual reproduction and maintaining a constant chromosome number across generations.</p><p>Understanding cell dynamics is crucial for defence aspirants as it forms the basis of genetics, pathology, and biotechnology. The regulation of the <b>Cell Cycle</b> (G1, S, G2, and M phases) prevents mutations and cancerous growths. Mastery of these concepts allows students to tackle complex questions on biological inheritance and cellular respiration frequently asked in the NDA and CDS science sections.</p>
  </section>

